From ad909197d8253ceab32f82b5420a00014e345801 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: build <build@localhost>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 22:07:16 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] Extensions: make Incognito work on phones

Desktop Android opens Incognito in its own window; a phone keeps regular and
Incognito tabs in one Activity with one AndroidBrowserWindow per profile.
Several pieces assumed one profile per window:

- The extension window controller was only created for the startup profile
  (upstream TODO crbug.com/491791326). With an Incognito tab in front,
  windows.getLastFocused() walked into the Incognito window, got a null
  controller and crashed the browser (any extension calling it, even one
  not allowed in Incognito). ChromeActivity now always gives the regular
  window its controller and adds one for the Incognito window whenever it is
  created, keyed to the Incognito TabModel so it is removed before that
  window is destroyed.

- Split-mode extensions (uBlock Origin) never got their Incognito background
  page: an incognito ProcessManager does not schedule its startup hosts, and
  the desktop ChromeProcessManagerDelegate that does so on window creation
  does not exist on Android. AndroidBrowserWindow now asks the incognito
  ProcessManager to create them when the Incognito window is created.

- The app-menu extension rows came from one ExtensionsToolbarCoordinator
  bound to the startup profile, so in Incognito the menu listed extensions
  not allowed there, tapping one ran it in the regular profile, and popups
  saw the regular tab. ToolbarManager now keeps one coordinator per profile
  (created on first use, the Incognito one removed with its TabModel), each
  inflating its own hidden container. Tablets keep the single regular
  coordinator because their container is visible in the toolbar.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
